Durarara!! AU
Chapter 4 – Part 1
The hallway outside the classroom had mostly cleared by the time Mikado found Anri.
He fell into step beside her with his usual earnest energy, already launching into a light conversation about the first day, the sudden class representative appointment, and how unexpected it had been that the sleeping transfer student ended up sharing the role with her. Anri responded in her usual soft, measured way—polite, a little reserved, but not unfriendly.
They didn’t get far.
Anri’s steps slowed first. Her gaze shifted past Mikado’s shoulder, and something in her posture changed. The quiet composure she usually carried tightened. Her shoulders drew in slightly, and the easy stillness she maintained around most people gave way to a subtle but clear discomfort.
Curtis, walking a short distance behind them after lingering in the classroom, saw it immediately.
His mismatched eyes tracked the direction of her attention.
A teacher was approaching down the hallway—Takashi Nasujima. Average height, ordinary appearance, the kind of man who blended into faculty until he decided not to. He carried himself with an easy, overfamiliar confidence that didn’t match the way Anri’s body language had shifted the moment she noticed him.
Curtis’s expression flattened.
“…That guy.”
The words came out quiet, almost under his breath, but the reaction sat deeper than simple dislike. Something about the teacher’s presence scraped against his instincts in a way that made his skin crawl. The way Nasujima’s eyes moved. The casualness that felt practiced. The way the air around Anri had changed the second he entered her line of sight.
Curtis had already decided.
Vermin.
He didn’t look away. He simply adjusted his path by a fraction, long black braids shifting against the blue uniform jacket as he continued forward, mismatched eyes fixed on the teacher with a calm that was significantly colder than anything he had shown during class.
Curtis stopped by his locker on the way out and shoved the blue uniform jacket inside without much thought. The white shirt and blue tie were enough for the walk home. The long black braids hung freely down his back as he left the building and started wandering the side streets around Raira with no particular destination in mind.
He hadn’t planned on getting involved in anything else today.
That plan lasted until he turned a corner and saw the familiar black bob and glasses.
Anri Sonohara stood near the mouth of a narrower street, shoulders slightly drawn in. Three girls in Raira uniforms had her partially boxed in—one with crossed arms and a sharp expression, another hovering close, the third hanging back but clearly part of the group. A guy in a yellow hoodie stood off to the side, watching with an ugly little smirk, like the whole thing was entertainment.
Curtis slowed.
His first instinct was to keep walking. Drama was easy to find in this city and even easier to avoid if you didn’t stop. He had already dealt with enough for one day—rooftops, class reps, strange conversations, and a teacher who made his skin crawl. Getting between a group of students and the quiet girl from his class was the kind of mess he preferred to leave alone.
His feet didn’t listen.
The moment he registered the way Anri’s posture had tightened—the same subtle discomfort he had seen earlier in the hallway with Nasujima—something colder settled behind his mismatched eyes. He adjusted his path without speeding up, long braids shifting against the white shirt as he walked straight into the space between Anri and the others.
He stopped right in the middle of them, calm and unhurried, and glanced sideways at her.
“Sup, Anri.” His voice was easy, almost casual. “Care to introduce me to your friends?”
The three girls and the guy in the yellow hoodie all turned toward the tall transfer student who had just inserted himself into their little circle like he belonged there. Anri looked up at him, surprise flickering briefly behind her glasses.
The shift in the group’s energy was almost immediate.
The sharp-eyed girl with crossed arms looked Curtis up and down, the hostility in her expression thinning as she took in the height, the braids, the mismatched eyes, and the calm way he had just walked into the middle of their circle. One of the others actually straightened her posture, a thin smile appearing. Even the third girl glanced at him with sudden interest. The guy in the yellow hoodie scowled, clearly not thrilled about losing the focus of the moment.
“Well damn,” the first girl said, voice turning sugary in a way that didn’t match her earlier tone. “Didn’t expect the new guy to be this fine. You free after this, foreigner?”
One of the others let out a light laugh, stepping half a pace closer.
“Yeah. You don’t have to waste time with her. We could show you around properly.”
Curtis didn’t smile.
He looked at each of them in turn, expression flat, then gave a short, dismissive breath.
“Yeah. None of you are my type.”
He lifted one hand and pointed directly at Anri without looking away from the girls.
“I like nice girls. Not ones who hide their faces behind a layer of makeup and a shitty attitude.”
The words landed clean and cold in the narrow street. The sugary expressions cracked. The guy in the yellow hoodie let out an offended noise. Anri, still standing slightly behind Curtis’s shoulder, went very still, a faint flush rising behind her glasses at being singled out so bluntly in front of the people who had been cornering her.
Curtis didn’t move. He simply stood there between them, waiting to see whether the group was smart enough to take the hint and leave.
A short distance down the street, two figures had stopped to watch.
Mikado stood with his hands half-clenched at his sides, frustration clear on his face as he stared at the tall transfer student who had once again stepped into a situation before anyone else could. First the class rep position. Now this. He had been working himself up to say something—anything—to get the girls and the guy in the yellow hoodie to back off from Anri, but Curtis had already walked straight into the middle of it and shut the whole thing down with a few cold sentences.
“I was going to…” Mikado started, voice low.
Masaomi put a hand on his shoulder, eyes narrowed as he watched the exchange.
“Don’t. Just watch.”
They weren’t the only ones observing.
A familiar voice drifted in from the side, light and amused.
“My, my. Leaving your friend out of the introductions, Masaomi? That’s rude.”
Izaya Orihara stepped into view with his usual unhurried confidence, hands in the pockets of his fur-collared coat, that sharp little smile already in place. His gaze flicked from Masaomi to Mikado, then toward the ongoing confrontation further up the street where Curtis still stood protectively near Anri.
Masaomi’s expression tightened immediately.
“Izaya.”
Mikado blinked, looking between them. The name, the coat, the way Masaomi’s entire posture had gone cautious—it clicked a second later. This was one of the two people his friend had specifically told him to avoid. The information broker. The one who smiled too much and treated people like entertainment.
The other name Masaomi had warned him about surfaced in the same breath.
Shizuo.
Mikado straightened slightly, suddenly a lot more aware of the company he was in, while Izaya’s smile only widened as he took in the full scene ahead of them—the cornered girl, the hostile group, and the tall foreigner with the braids who had already decided the situation belonged to him.
Haruko’s sugary expression vanished the moment the rejection landed fully.
The blonde girl’s face twisted. Youko (the brown-haired one) clicked her tongue, arms tightening across her chest. Akie, the one with the pink hair and the little wing design on her bag, scowled and muttered something under her breath. All three of them had clearly expected the tall, handsome foreigner to at least play along. Being dismissed so cleanly—and in favor of the quiet girl they had been targeting—stung more than they wanted to admit.
Haruko jerked her chin toward the guy in the yellow hoodie.
“Hiroshi.”
The other two girls made similar motions, stepping back just enough to give him room.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes shifted to the approaching figure without any change in expression. He already had the measure of him. Loud. Chest puffed out. The kind of guy who talked bigger than he could back up and borrowed other people’s reputations when his own wasn’t enough. A pretend thug who needed an audience to feel dangerous.
Hiroshi stopped right in Curtis’s space, close enough that the height difference became obvious. He had to look up.
“You got a real mouth on you for a transfer student,” Hiroshi said, voice raised for the benefit of the girls behind him. “You think you can just walk up and talk shit? You got any idea who you’re messing with?”
He jabbed a finger toward Curtis’s chest without quite touching him.
“I’m with the Dollars, asshole. You keep running that mouth and you’re gonna regret it. We don’t take kindly to outsiders acting tough on our streets.”
The threat hung in the air between them, backed by nothing but volume and the three girls watching with vengeful interest. Anri remained quiet behind Curtis’s shoulder. Further down the street, Mikado, Masaomi, and Izaya continued to observe.
Curtis looked down at Hiroshi, completely unmoved.
Curtis looked down at Hiroshi for a long, flat second.
Then he blinked once, slow and unimpressed.
“Sorry. I zoned out.” His voice stayed completely even. “Did you say something?”
The silence that followed was almost worse than a direct insult.
Hiroshi’s face went red in real time. The finger he had been jabbing toward Curtis’s chest froze in the air. Behind him, Haruko’s mouth fell open. Youko made a sharp, offended sound. Akie’s expression twisted between disbelief and secondhand embarrassment.
“You—!” Hiroshi’s voice cracked upward. “You fucking listening to me, you tall piece of shit?! I said I’m with the Dollars! You keep running your mouth and—”
He stepped even closer, trying to use volume and proximity to reclaim the ground he had just lost. The act only made the height difference more obvious. Curtis still hadn’t moved. He hadn’t raised his voice. He hadn’t even shifted his weight. He simply stood there looking at Hiroshi like the entire threat had been background noise.
Further down the street, Izaya’s smile widened with open delight. Masaomi muttered something under his breath. Mikado watched with a mix of awe and rising tension.
Hiroshi’s hands curled into fists at his sides, the yellow hoodie shifting as he tried to decide whether pure noise was still going to be enough.
Hiroshi’s hand went to his pocket.
The knife came out in a short, ugly arc—cheap folding blade, the kind bought more for the image than the edge. He thrust it forward, trying to force Curtis back with the threat alone.
Curtis sighed.
The sound was quiet, almost disappointed.
What happened next was too fast for most of the people watching to fully track. One moment the knife was in Hiroshi’s grip. The next, Curtis’s hand had already closed around the wrist, redirected the momentum, and taken the weapon cleanly. There was no wasted motion. No dramatic flourish. Just efficient, practiced control.
Hiroshi barely had time to process that he was empty-handed before the blade flashed once at head height.
A clean, horizontal pass.
The top of Hiroshi’s hair parted in a single smooth cut. A thick section of it fell away and scattered across the pavement between them. Not a scratch on the scalp. Just the hair, gone.
Curtis flipped the knife once in his hand, examined the edge, then snapped the blade sideways against his own palm with a sharp, final crack. The cheap metal broke with almost no resistance. He let the pieces drop to the ground.
“Cheap metal.” He turned the remaining handle slightly, inspecting the break. “Nice cut, though. Smooov.”
The street went dead quiet.
Hiroshi stood frozen, one hand slowly rising to the newly uneven top of his head. When his fingers found the missing section, his face went from red to bloodless. Haruko, Youko, and Akie stared with identical expressions of stunned disbelief—none of them had expected the tall transfer student to respond with that level of precision or calm.
Anri’s eyes were wide behind her glasses, fixed on Curtis’s back.
Further down the street, Mikado’s mouth had fallen open. Masaomi’s expression had gone tight and serious. Izaya, on the other hand, looked like someone had just handed him the best entertainment of the week—his smile sharp with pure, delighted interest.
Hiroshi finally found his voice. It came out higher than before.
“You… you crazy bastard—!”
Curtis looked at Hiroshi the same way someone might look at a buzzing insect that had already been swatted once.
“Now we’re resorting to name-calling.” His voice stayed flat, almost bored. “What are we, grade schoolers? You’re out of your league. Piss off.”
He didn’t raise his voice. He didn’t step forward. He simply stood there over the broken knife and the scattered hair, long black braids hanging still against the white shirt, mismatched eyes waiting to see whether Hiroshi was stupid enough to try anything else.
The last of the fight drained out of the yellow-hoodied boy in real time. Whatever bravado the Dollars name had given him was gone, replaced by the sharp understanding that he had just been disarmed, humiliated, and dismissed in front of the girls he was trying to impress. His hand stayed on the uneven patch of hair at the top of his head like he still couldn’t believe it was real.
Haruko was the first to break.
“We’re leaving,” she snapped, already grabbing Youko’s arm. Akie followed without needing to be told, face pale. The three of them turned and walked fast, not quite running but close.
Hiroshi lingered half a second longer, mouth opening and closing like he wanted one last threat and couldn’t find one that wouldn’t make things worse. Then he spun and hurried after the girls, yellow hoodie disappearing down the street.
Curtis watched them go until they rounded the corner. Only then did he glance back at Anri.
Further down the street, Izaya looked like he was having the time of his life.
Curtis turned fully toward Anri once the last of the group had disappeared around the corner. The cold edge he had shown Hiroshi was already gone, replaced by the same calm he usually carried.
“You good, Anri?”
Anri stood still for a moment, hands lightly clasped in front of her, eyes moving from the broken pieces of the knife on the ground to the scattered hair, then up to Curtis’s face. The residual tension in her shoulders eased only slowly.
“…Yes,” she answered quietly. “I’m fine. Thanks to you.”
She hesitated, glancing once more at the evidence of how quickly the situation had been shut down.
“You didn’t have to step in,” she added, voice still soft. “But… I’m glad you did. They’ve been following me for a few days. I wasn’t sure what they wanted this time.”
Her gaze lifted to his mismatched eyes, searching them briefly.
“You’re… very capable, Curtis-kun.”
Curtis gave a small shrug, the long black braids shifting with the movement.
“Nah. I just happened to pass by.” His tone stayed easy, as if disarming someone and sending a whole group packing was nothing worth noting. “Want me to walk you to where you need to go? It won’t be a problem.”
Anri opened her mouth to answer—
—but never got the chance.
Three sets of footsteps approached from the direction the earlier observers had been standing. Izaya was in front, hands in the pockets of his fur-collared coat, that sharp, delighted smile still firmly in place. Masaomi walked a half-step behind him with a more guarded expression. Mikado brought up the rear, still looking like he was processing everything he had just witnessed.
Izaya stopped a few paces away, eyes bright as he took in the broken knife pieces, the scattered hair, and the tall transfer student standing protectively near Anri.
“Well,” Izaya said cheerfully. “That was educational.”
Izaya’s smile didn’t fade as he looked from the broken knife on the ground to Curtis.
“Disarmed him, cut his hair, snapped the blade, and sent the whole group packing without even raising your voice.” He tilted his head, clearly enjoying himself. “All while looking completely bored. I have to say, I’m impressed. Most people at least break a sweat when a knife comes out.”
Masaomi shot Izaya a warning look, then turned his attention to Curtis. His tone was more measured.
“You didn’t have to go that far. Cutting his hair was… a choice.”
Mikado lingered a step behind them, still visibly processing. His eyes kept flicking between Curtis, the scattered hair on the pavement, and Anri, who remained quietly at Curtis’s side.
“That was… really fast,” Mikado said, a little awkwardly. “I didn’t even see how you took the knife.”
Curtis regarded the three of them without much change in expression. The same calm he had shown during the confrontation was still there, though his mismatched eyes lingered on Izaya a fraction longer than the others—recognition without warmth.
Anri glanced up at Curtis, then at the new arrivals, her usual soft composure returning now that the immediate threat had passed. She didn’t step away from him.
Izaya’s gaze sharpened with fresh interest as he focused fully on the tall transfer student.
“We weren’t properly introduced earlier,” he said lightly. “You’re the one from last night, aren’t you? The masked foreigner who left cracks in the sidewalk and refused to give his name. And now here you are again, collecting trouble in broad daylight.”
He smiled wider.
“I think I’d like to know what to call you.” 
Curtis’s mismatched eyes narrowed slightly as he looked at Izaya.
“So you know it’s me. My braids weren’t out when we met. Therefore you’ve been watching me… or had someone watching me.”
The statement landed flat and certain. He didn’t phrase it as a question.
Then his gaze shifted to Masaomi, colder than before.
“And blondie—don’t tell me how far I should take things. Especially when you and your friend just stood there and did nothing.” A short pause. “Don’t lecture me.”
The air between the group tightened.
Izaya’s smile didn’t disappear, but it changed—sharpened with genuine delight rather than simple amusement. Being read so quickly only seemed to interest him more.
“Observant,” he said softly. “I do like that about you.”
Masaomi’s expression darkened. Being called out in front of Mikado and Anri clearly didn’t sit well, especially with the added “blondie.” His shoulders tensed.
“We were assessing the situation,” he said, voice tighter than before. “Jumping in half-cocked gets people hurt. You got lucky he only had a cheap knife.”
Mikado looked uncomfortable, caught between agreeing with his friend and the fact that Curtis had a point. They had just watched. He opened his mouth, then closed it again without finding anything useful to say.
Anri remained quiet at Curtis’s side, her eyes moving between the three of them, clearly noting the sudden shift in tone.
Izaya, meanwhile, looked more entertained than ever.
Curtis’s voice stayed level, but the edge underneath it sharpened.
“If you’re scared of being stabbed, say so. If jumping into a situation makes you scared, then say so. But don’t make an excuse saying ‘jumping in half-cocked gets people hurt.’”
He glanced once toward Anri, then back to Masaomi.
“You’re lucky I was nearby. Otherwise she would’ve gotten hurt. So shut up unless you’ve got something useful to say.”
A short, cold pause.
“Or is being a flirty pig all you’re good at?”
The words hit the group like a slap.
Masaomi’s face went rigid. The casual composure he usually carried around Mikado cracked, replaced by a flash of real anger. Being called a coward was one thing. The last line—hitting the exact reputation he knew he had—landed harder than the rest.
“The hell did you just say to me?” Masaomi took a half-step forward before catching himself, jaw tight.
Mikado’s eyes widened. He looked between his friend and Curtis like the situation had just slipped completely out of any script he understood.
“H-hey, wait—” he started, but didn’t seem to know how to finish.
Izaya, on the other hand, looked like he had just been given a front-row seat to his favorite kind of entertainment. His smile was wide and sharp with pure enjoyment.
“Oh, I really like him,” Izaya murmured, almost to himself.
Anri remained still at Curtis’s side, quiet but clearly aware of how quickly the tension had escalated. Her gaze flicked toward Masaomi, then back to Curtis, as if trying to decide whether this was going to turn into something worse.
Masaomi moved.
The punch came fast—clean, practiced, the kind of strike from someone who had been in real fights before. It connected solidly against Curtis’s jaw with a sharp, audible impact.
Curtis’s head turned a fraction with the force.
That was all.
He didn’t stagger. He didn’t step back. He didn’t even blink harder than usual. He simply stood there and took it like the hit had landed against something far denser than flesh and bone.
Masaomi, on the other hand, immediately hissed and pulled his hand back, face tightening in pain. The impact had clearly hurt him more than the target. He shook his wrist once, staring at Curtis with a mix of anger and sudden, reluctant understanding.
Curtis slowly turned his head back to face him fully, mismatched eyes calm and cold.
“The first one’s free.”
His voice remained even, almost quiet.
“I suggest you take your own advice and think long and hard about what your next move will be.” A short pause. “I suggest apologizing.”
The street felt smaller.
Mikado had gone pale, eyes wide as he looked from Masaomi’s injured hand to Curtis’s completely unharmed face. Izaya’s delighted expression had sharpened into something closer to fascination. Anri’s fingers had tightened slightly around the strap of her bag, but she still didn’t step away from Curtis’s side.
Masaomi stood there holding his wrist, breathing a little harder than before, the reality of what he had just hit settling in.
Curtis didn’t move.
He simply stood there, mismatched eyes fixed on Masaomi, and asked the only question that mattered now.
“So?”
The single word hung in the air, quiet and expectant.
Masaomi’s jaw worked. His injured hand was still cradled against his side, the sting of the impact a clear reminder that hitting the tall transfer student had been a mistake. Pride and pain warred across his face as Mikado looked on with open anxiety and Izaya watched like the entire exchange was pure entertainment.
For a few long seconds, Masaomi said nothing.
Then he exhaled through his nose, sharp and reluctant.
“…Tch.” He looked away first, then forced the words out. “Fine. Sorry.”
It wasn’t graceful. It wasn’t particularly sincere. But it was an apology.
Mikado visibly relaxed a fraction. Izaya’s smile widened, clearly pleased by how the power dynamic had shifted. Anri remained quiet at Curtis’s side, her gaze moving between the two of them as the tension finally began to thin—if only slightly.
Curtis regarded Masaomi for another moment, as if weighing whether the apology was enough.
Then he gave a small, single nod.
Curtis held out his hand.
The gesture was steady, without any theatrics.
“Then so am I,” he said. “I know your type. Ladies’ man. You’re not a pig… but if you’re going to spit fire, be prepared to get burned back.”
The offer sat between them—an acknowledgment and a warning in the same breath.
Masaomi looked at the outstretched hand for a second longer than necessary, still nursing his wrist. Pride made him hesitate. Practicality won. He reached out and took it, the handshake firm but brief.
“…Yeah,” Masaomi muttered. “Noted.”
He pulled his hand back and flexed his fingers once, testing the lingering ache. The anger hadn’t fully left his face, but the immediate heat of the confrontation had cooled into something more wary.
Mikado let out a quiet breath he had clearly been holding. Izaya looked mildly disappointed that the tension had dropped, though the sharp interest in his eyes remained firmly fixed on Curtis. Anri’s shoulders eased by a fraction as the two young men settled into a tense but workable truce.
Curtis released the handshake and slipped his hand back into his pocket, as if the entire exchange had already been filed away.
Izaya let out a soft, delighted laugh.
“How civilized,” he said, clapping his hands together once in mock applause. “A clean little exchange of apologies and life lessons right here on the sidewalk. I’m touched, really.”
He stepped closer, that sharp smile still firmly in place as he looked between Curtis and Masaomi.
“Though I have to admit, I’m a little disappointed. I was hoping Blondie here would try for a second punch. The look on his face when the first one hurt him more was priceless.” His eyes flicked to Masaomi with open amusement before settling back on Curtis. “And you… you just stood there and took it like someone hitting a wall. Then calmly suggested he apologize. No rage. No panic. Just quiet control.”
Izaya tilted his head, studying him with fresh interest.
“You’re full of surprises, nameless foreigner. First the masked performance, then the concrete, then the rooftop girl, and now this. You keep collecting interesting moments.” His smile widened. “I do hope you plan on sticking around Ikebukuro for a while. It would be such a waste if someone this entertaining disappeared too quickly.”
The warning came too late.
A full metal trash can came flying down the street like a missile and slammed into both Curtis and Izaya with bone-jarring force. The impact sent them stumbling apart—Izaya with a sharp, undignified yelp, Curtis taking the hit solidly across the shoulder and side before the can crashed to the pavement and rolled away, spilling garbage across the sidewalk.
Masaomi’s face lit up with pure, unfiltered joy.
“Holy shit~!”
“Curtis-kun!” Anri’s voice cut through the noise, sharp with rare alarm as she stepped forward.
From further down the street, a furious shout tore through the air.
“IZAYAAA!!!!! WHAT ARE YOU DOING IN IKEBUKURO?!”
Shizuo Heiwajima stormed into view, blond hair wild, bartender vest over his white shirt, and murder written across every line of his face. His eyes locked onto Izaya with pure, undiluted rage, completely ignoring everyone else for the moment.
Izaya, half-sprawled from the impact, already had his hands up in a familiar mix of pain and theatrical survival instinct.
Curtis straightened slowly from the hit, one hand briefly touching his shoulder where the trash can had connected. There was no blood. No broken posture. Just a faint tightness in his expression as the long black braids settled back into place. He glanced from the dented trash can to the approaching blond force of nature, then to Izaya, mismatched eyes narrowing slightly at the sudden escalation.
Curtis rolled his shoulder once where the trash can had hit him, long black braids shifting as he straightened fully. The calm he had carried through every confrontation so far finally cracked—just enough to let real irritation through.
“Who the hell just throws trash cans?” His voice was sharper than it had been all day. “I wasn’t before… but I’m starting to get pissed!”
Shizuo’s furious advance stuttered for half a second as he registered that someone other than Izaya was yelling. His eyes flicked to Curtis, taking in the height, the braids, the fact that the tall foreigner had taken a full trash can hit and was still standing like it was only mildly annoying.
“The hell’s your problem?!” Shizuo barked back. “That flea’s the one I’m after—stay out of it if you don’t wanna get broken!”
Izaya, still half on the ground, seized the opening like a lifeline.
“Yes! Listen to the angry bartender!” he called, already scrambling backward. “This is a private matter between me and the monster! No need for the mysterious foreigner to get involved—especially when he’s already been used as collateral once!”
Masaomi looked torn between satisfaction at Izaya’s suffering and fresh concern at the rising temperature. Mikado had gone pale again. Anri remained close to Curtis, eyes moving warily between him and the blond man who had just weaponized public sanitation.
Shizuo cracked his knuckles, attention splitting between Izaya and the transfer student who was now glaring at him with mismatched eyes.
Curtis didn’t answer Shizuo right away.
His breathing had changed—deeper, heavier, the kind of controlled pull of air that came when something under the surface was starting to push back. The irritation in his mismatched eyes had sharpened into something hotter. One hand flexed at his side, then curled slowly into a fist as he rolled his shoulder again, testing the lingering impact of the trash can.
Anri noticed first.
“Curtis-kun… are you okay?”
Her voice was quiet but clear, edged with genuine concern as she stepped half a pace closer, eyes searching his face. The usual soft composure she carried was strained by the sudden violence and the way Curtis’s demeanor had shifted from cold control to something visibly heavier.
Shizuo narrowed his eyes at the change, still coiled for a fight. Izaya, from his safer distance, watched with renewed interest—the foreigner’s breathing, the tension in his frame, the way the air around him felt different than it had a minute ago.
Curtis’s gaze stayed locked ahead, chest rising and falling with that heavier rhythm, as if he were deciding whether to keep the lid on whatever had started to surface.
